==========================================================================================================================
VERDICT OF THE ADVERSARIAL PASS:  THE CLAIM IS REFUTED AS STATED.
==========================================================================================================================
  THE ARITHMETIC IS CORRECT AND REPRODUCES INDEPENDENTLY (V1-V4).  Matched separation +0.064 dex under the
  exact sphere average, +0.157 dex under FM12 eq. 60, difference 0.093 dex, ratio 1.4; and it is the
  same on both a_0 footings, which g05 never checked (its scan() hard-codes the canonical a_0).  Nothing is
  wrong with the numbers.  What is wrong is what they are called.

  THE 0.093 dex IS NOT AN EXTERNAL-FIELD-PRESCRIPTION SYSTEMATIC (V7b, V7c, V6).  Three independent ways:

   1. THE MATCHED SAMPLE HAS ALMOST NO EXTERNAL FIELD IN IT.  Matching to SPARC's acceleration coverage
      selects the 14 objects with the HIGHEST internal acceleration, and every one of them has x_e < x_i
      (max x_e/x_i = 0.43, median 0.06).  Switching the external field off ENTIRELY moves the
      exact prediction by a median 0.0001 dex and at most 0.0157 dex.  The claimed 'external-field
      prescription systematic' is 477 times the whole external-field effect it purports to prescribe.
      A spread that persists when the effect is switched off is not a systematic of that effect.

   2. THE 1-D FORMULA IS NOT A VALUE THE FIELD EVER TAKES.  For 13 of the 14 matched objects it lies BELOW
      the exact QUMOND radial source's MINIMUM over every direction on the sphere.  It is not the same field
      evaluated with a different directional weighting; it is outside the field's range.

   3. THE TWO DISAGREE ON THE ORDER OF THE EFFECT.  Deep-MOND: FM12 = sqrt(x_i+x_e) - sqrt(x_e), deficit
      first order in sqrt(x_e/x_i); the exact sphere average's linear term cancels by symmetry, deficit
      second order.  Measured over four decades: exact ~ (x_e/x_i)^2.00, FM12 ~ (x_e/x_i)^0.49.  The 1-D
      formula carries a spurious sqrt(x_e) suppression that never switches off, and THAT, not any physical
      ambiguity, is the whole of the 0.093 dex.

  AND THE LOOPHOLE THAT COULD HAVE SAVED IT IS CLOSED (V7).  g05's own weakest-link defence -- that the
  sphere average is only exact for an isotropic tracer -- is a real overclaim: the Wolf et al. 2010 estimator
  assumes a spherically symmetric potential and the external field breaks that.  Had the anisotropy been
  large it would have supplied a genuine irreducible systematic.  It is a median 7 per cent peak-to-peak
  over the matched objects, far too small to be the 0.093 dex.

  TWO FURTHER OVERSTATEMENTS.  (V5) 'ratio 1.4' is a ratio of two differences of medians of 14 objects; it
  exceeds 1 in only 65 per cent of bootstrap resamples, 16-84 range 0.50 to 3.55.  (V9) 'changes SIGN
  and lands four sigma on the WRONG side' is true only of f09's max() branch, which g05's own check P3 shows
  is kinked and which g05 discards everywhere else; between the two treatments the claim itself calls
  defensible the separation is positive both times, +0.064 to +0.157 dex.

  WHAT SURVIVES, AND IT IS THE CONCLUSION RATHER THAN THE ARGUMENT (V10).  Under the exact prescription
  alone -- no appeal to any systematic -- the matched separation is +0.064 dex, 0.93 sigma, permutation
  p = 0.08 on 14 pressure objects, and the top of the whole prescription range is 2.1 sigma.  'Not a
  measurement' is forced by the statistics and the sample size.  It is NOT forced by, and should not be
  argued from, a prescription systematic that the physics says is not there.

  QUOTE INSTEAD:  'With the exact QUMOND sphere average the matched separation is +0.06 dex, 0.93 sigma,
  p = 0.10 on 14 objects and 2.9 sigma only under a 0.40 dex matching window that re-admits objects with no
  rotating counterpart.  The published 1-D external-field formula gives +0.16 dex, but it under-predicts the
  exact field everywhere on the sphere for these near-isolated objects and its departure is its own
  convergence error, not a systematic of the framework.'

  ONE FURTHER PHYSICS ERROR IN THE SAME FILE, load-bearing for a DIFFERENT g05 claim (V8): check A1c reads
  its measured residual slope of -0.498 as the mechanical identity of the ISOLATED deep-MOND limit,
  g ~ sqrt(x_i).  55 of 79 of these dwarfs have x_e > x_i and are EXTERNAL-FIELD dominated, where the
  prediction is LINEAR in x_i and the identity slope is -1.  Computing the identity instead of asserting it
  -- hold g_obs fixed and refit through the same prescription -- gives -0.878, not -0.500.  So the measured
  -0.498 is NOT the no-information value: it is 0.38 dex/dex away from it, and A1c's reading of its
  own slope as 'a mechanical identity' is wrong in the direction of understating what it measured.

